% ============================================================================
% reference-line-hemisphere-split — for the two lines whose SAME cited
% source also names the hemispheres they divide, that split
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `reference-lines.adj` (`reference_line(line,
% marks)`, ONE degree-marking property per line: equator, prime_meridian,
% tropic_of_cancer, tropic_of_capricorn, arctic_circle, antarctic_circle).
% That table's own `source` and `cites` fields already quote, VERBATIM, a
% SECOND fact for two of those six lines — which pair of hemispheres each
% line divides the Earth into — but the `marks`-only schema (which only
% carries the degree fact) had no room for it:
%
%   equator → northern_southern
%     source: "The equator is the most well known parallel. At 0 degrees
%     latitude, it equally divides the Earth into the Northern and Southern
%     hemispheres." (NOAA National Ocean Service, "What is latitude?")
%
%   prime_meridian → eastern_western
%     cites: "The prime meridian, which runs through Greenwich, England, has
%     a longitude of 0 degrees. It divides the Earth into the eastern and
%     western hemispheres." (NOAA National Ocean Service, "What is
%     longitude?")
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? reference_line_hemisphere_split(equator, $Hemispheres)   % northern_southern
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `reference-lines.adj`'s own `reference_line`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `reference_line_hemisphere_split(line, hemispheres)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the hemisphere split WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on every other reference line
% `reference-lines.adj` carries, since neither of the two tropics nor either
% polar circle has a hemisphere-split fact stated in their own cited spans
% (the tropics and polar circles mark latitude limits, not a hemisphere
% division).
%
% Only TWO of `reference-lines.adj`'s six lines have a genuinely distinct
% hemisphere-split fact in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is
% deliberately narrow rather than inventing a split the source does not
% state. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a hemisphere pair and recall
% which line divides it:
%
%     ? reference_line_hemisphere_split($Line, eastern_western)   % prime_meridian
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME equator and prime-meridian
% spans already quoted inside `reference-lines.adj`'s own `source` and
% `cites` fields, from the SAME two NOAA National Ocean Service pages that
% table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding
% the hemisphere-split half of each already-verified quote. `trust
% authoritative` — the same tier `reference-lines.adj` already carries (NOAA,
% a U.S. federal .gov source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table reference_line_hemisphere_split {
    columns line, hemispheres

    row (equator, northern_southern)          % NOAA "What is latitude?" (latitude.html)
    row (prime_meridian, eastern_western)      % NOAA "What is longitude?" (longitude.html)

    source "The equator is the most well known parallel. At 0 degrees latitude, it equally divides the Earth into the Northern and Southern hemispheres."
    locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/latitude.html"
    trust authoritative

    cites "The prime meridian, which runs through Greenwich, England, has a longitude of 0 degrees. It divides the Earth into the eastern and western hemispheres." locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/longitude.html"
}
